  "text": "Solutions 2 and 3 do not hold up. Solution 2 takes $\\Phi$ to be $1$, $x$, $x^2$, or $x^2-1$ and then counts cases of $B$ by hand, but it never checks that these cases are distinct under the remaining affine maps. Solution 3 splits the cases differently (4+4+4+2) and gives no justification for why the $\\Phi=x^2-1$ branch collapses to two families when the other branches have four. Both totals (13 and 14) come from unverified sketches, so I don't trust either.\n\nSolution 1 gives 44 and attributes it to Bouakkaz and Maroni's description of Laguerre–Hahn forms of class zero. I can't verify this here because I have no search or code tools in this session. The number does match my recollection of that literature, and it is the only candidate that cites a specific source rather than a sketched count. Its reasoning is shaky, though, since it reconstructs the source from memory and hedges several times.\n\nOn balance, Solution 1 is the most plausible of the three, but my confidence is low and I haven't checked it against the paper.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
